          "path": "_m/d/j/u/m/indian-springs-state-park-wp/negus-portrait-of-william-mcin.jpg",
          "filename": "Negus, Portrait of William McIntosh, Chief of the Lower Creek Indians in Georgia, 1821.jpg",
          "caption": "Portrait of William McIntosh, Chief of the Lower Creek Indians in Georgia, attributed to Nathan Negus and Joseph Negus, 1821. (Formerly attributed to Washington Allston.)  In a journal entry dated April 23, 1821, Nathan Negus recorded, \"We started for the Indian Springs [Georgia] for the purpose of painting Gen'l [General] McIntosh's portrait. . . . The next day we travelled through the wilderness — at sundown we arrived at the place of destination — Gen'l McIntosh was too full of business to attend to his portrait and of consequence we waited several days without doing anything — here we saw about 12 hundred Indians.\" Source: Typescript of Negus journal entry, as quoted from Agnes Dodd to Peter Brannon, undated letter, Alabama Department of History and Archives, director’s files, as quoted in Naomi Slipp, \"Traversing Two Cultures: A Portrait of William McIntosh, Southern Slave Owner and Lower Creek Chief,\" Panorama: Journal of the Association of Historians of American\nArt 6, no. 2 (Fall 2020).",
          "alt": "Portrait of William McIntosh, Chief of the Lower Creek Indians in Georgia, attributed to Nathan Negus and Joseph Negus, 1821. (Formerly attributed to Washington Allston.)  In a journal entry dated April 23, 1821, Nathan Negus recorded, \"We started for the Indian Springs [Georgia] for the purpose of painting Gen'l [General] McIntosh's portrait. . . . The next day we travelled through the wilderness — at sundown we arrived at the place of destination — Gen'l McIntosh was too full of business to attend to his portrait and of consequence we waited several days without doing anything — here we saw about 12 hundred Indians.\" Source: Typescript of Negus journal entry, as quoted from Agnes Dodd to Peter Brannon, undated letter, Alabama Department of History and Archives, director’s files, as quoted in Naomi Slipp, \"Traversing Two Cultures: A Portrait of William McIntosh, Southern Slave Owner and Lower Creek Chief,\" Panorama: Journal of the Association of Historians of American\nArt 6, no. 2 (Fall 2020).",
